  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe current algorithm focuses on local optimization using SLSQP and minimax heuristics, which may get stuck in local minima. I propose a multi-resolution gradient descent approach that leverages the Fast Fourier Transform for more efficient optimization. This method will:\n1. Start with the initial parent solution at n=256\n2. Use gradient descent to minimize the Chebyshev norm of the convolution\n3. Gradually increase resolution using interpolation\n4. Include multi-start optimization to escape local minima\n5. Use a penalty function for boundary constraints\n\nThe central idea is to frame the problem as a finite-dimensional optimization with linear constraints (integral constraint) and use gradient-based methods for their efficiency. The FFT-based convolution computation allows for constant-time operation on the entire grid, making gradient calculation feasible.\n\nMathematical specification:\nLet h=(h1,h2,...,hn) be a real vector with:\n0 \u2264 hi \u2264 1 for all i\nsum_{i=1}^n hi = n/4 (since \u222bh dx = 0.5 and dx=2/n)\n\nThe objective is:\nf(h) = \\max_{k} (2/n * ||h * (1-h)||_{k})\n\nWhere * denotes convolution and ||.||_{k} is the L1 norm at lag k.\n\nConstraints handling:\nWe use an augmented Lagrangian method with:\n- Box constraints: 0 \u2264 hi \u2264 1\n- Integral constraint: sum hi = n/4\n\nDimensionality specification:\nStarts at 256 dimensions. For each optimization step:\n1. Gradient descent updates: optimized_vector <- optimized_vector - \u03b1 * \u2207f\n2. Each update is followed by projection onto feasible set:\n   - Clip values to [0,1]\n   - Adjust sum to n/4 using gradient projection\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Setup:\n   - Read initial_h_values (n=256)\n   - Define compute_objective(h, n): \n        corr = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')\n        return (2/n) * np.max(corr)\n   - Derivative computation using automatic differentiation\n   - Gradient projection for constraint handling\n\n2. Optimization loop for n=256,512,1024:\n   - Precompute FFT of h and (1-h) for each optimization step\n   - Update: hnew = h - \u03b3 * \u2207f(h)\n   - Project: \n        clipped = np.clip(hnew, 0, 1)\n        sum_adjust = (n/4 - sum(clipped)) / n\n        h_feas = clipped + sum_adjust * np.ones(n)  (ensure sum exactly n/4)\n   - Terminate if |gradient| < tol or time expired\n\n3. Multi-start optimization (3 starts per resolution):\n   - Use different initial conditions at each resolution:\n        - Parent interpolated to new resolution\n        - Randomized bit-like patterns\n        - Smoothed versions of parent\n\n4. Early termination if:\n   - Remaining time < 10s per resolution\n   - No improvement seen for 10 iterations\n\nSearch choices:\n- Start n's: [256, 512, 1024]\n- Step size \u03b3: adaptive (start with 0.01, halve if improvement stalls)\n- Gradient threshold: 1e-3\n- Max iterations: 500 per resolution\n\nValidation:\n   - Check 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and sum h = n/4\n   - Recompute objective on final candidate\n   - Low-cost feasibility checks during gradient projection\n\nCompute budget:\n- n=256: ~ 600s\n- n=512: ~500s\n- n=1024: ~200s\n- Total estimate: 1300s\n\nImplementation details:\n   - Use np.pad for FFT-based convolution (not circular)\n   - Gradient calculation: finite differences (\u03b5=1e-4) or automatic differentiation\n   - Constraint tolerance: 1e-6\n   - Projection ensures integral constraint (not just clipping)\n   - Objective evaluation: max_{|k|<n} |h * (1-h)|_{k} \n\nLet's proceed with gradient descent implementation for each resolution, scaling the learning rate and using early termination.",
